cture of “every man.” --- ### 2. Training isn’t for the patterns; it’s for the individuals in front of you Even if, for the sake of argument, there *were* some statistical tendency for men to take more risks or overlook details, a training program can’t treat people as averages. If you dumb down the material because you expect “guy behavior,” you will: - Bore or insult the many men who *don’t* fit that pattern, and who will immediately check out. - Fail to give adequate support to those who genuinely need clearer guidance, because you’re relying on oversimplification instead of clear scaffolding. - Potentially reinforce the very behavior you’re trying to prevent: if the training communicates “we expect you to be dumb,” you can lower people’s own standards for themselves. The goal of training is to meet everyone where they are and bring them to competence. That requires assuming they *can* learn, not pre-emptively lowering the bar based on their gender. --- ### 3. “Simple” for the right reasons is fine; “simple because you’re a guy” is not If you’ve observed that the men you work with tend to skip reading, gloss over details, or jump straight into action, then **use that insight to design better training**, not to make it insulting. For example: - Start with a clear, practical problem they’ll face, so they see immediate relevance. - Break procedures into step-by-step, no-fluff instructions. - Use checklists and hands-on practice instead of long text blocks. - Build in “why this safety step matters” to connect reasoning to action. These are all just sound instructional design principles for an audience that may be impatient or hands-on. They work for everyone, regardless of gender, and they don’t rely on calling anyone dumb. --- ### 4. The bottom line I’m not saying your life experience is invalid or that you haven’t seen a real pattern. What I’m saying is: basing a professional training approach on the idea that “men act dumb” will produce worse outcomes than treatin